From ca2d41aaad0fcdaad061e59c5232c28ced5ce673 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Boyd <danieljboyd@icloud.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 14:15:20 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] x11: load libGL at run time instead of linking it

The backend bundle linked libGL whenever it was built with GLX, so every
app, OpenGL or not, failed to start where no libGL is installed.  It now
opens libGL.so.1, or glvnd's libGLX.so.0, the first time an app asks for
an OpenGL pixel format or context, and looks up the GLX and GL functions
it calls (Source/x11/XGGLLoader.m, Headers/x11/XGGLLoader.h): a table of
pointers typed by the <GL/glx.h> prototypes, filled once under
pthread_once, and macros that send the existing calls through it, so the
call sites don't change.  With glvnd's libGLX the GL functions come from
glXGetProcAddressARB.

When neither library loads, or one lacks a function, the backend logs one
line saying why, -glContextClass and -glPixelFormatClass return nil, and
XGGLPixelFormat and XGGLContext refuse to initialise, so
NSOpenGLPixelFormat and NSOpenGLContext give nil and an NSOpenGLView has
no context; everything else works as before.

configure still checks for libGL and <GL/glx.h> (the headers are needed
to build) but no longer adds -lGL; it adds -ldl only where dlopen isn't
in libc.

Tests/x11/openglLoad.m checks that starting the backend doesn't load
libGL, that asking for a pixel format does, and, run where libGL.so.1
and libGLX.so.0 can't be found, that OpenGL reports unavailable while a
window with an NSOpenGLView shows and closes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Ugm29zQymfPxERSSTrHhey
